#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284405 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $4\\,kg$ at a rate of $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=4(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})=(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+{e}^{4}-(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =4+{e}^{4}-0-1 \\\\  & =({e}^{4}+3)\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284406 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(8-x)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(24-\\frac{9}{2})-(8-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =3{v}_{1}^{2}-108 \\\\ 3{v}_{1} & =120 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm \\sqrt{40} \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284407 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=3.5\\cdot 2{x}^{3}=7{x}^{3}\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}7{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(16-0) \\\\  & =28\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284408 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+x)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{x}){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}-2) \\\\  & =9\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284409 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $7\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(2x+3)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(9+9)-(1+3) \\\\  & =14\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot (2\\sqrt{5})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =70-\\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =56 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284410 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $({x}^{3}-4)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=3$, and the particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=3$ to $x=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}({x}^{3}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-4x){|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{625}{4}-20)-(\\frac{81}{4}-12) \\\\  & =128\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =200-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $6\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284411 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $3x\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{2}\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{125}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=5-4=1\\,m/s.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284412 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude ${t}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{4}(0)^{4}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)=\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4})=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284413 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(1+{t}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=1\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=0+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(1)=1+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284414 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2{e}^{-t}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-2{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-2{e}^{-0}+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2-2{e}^{-t}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=1\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(1)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284499 :question/problem "![](images/61/6116ecf00a26d9828b238879b61385c1296f33ff863dbb8337a3b22be5981f10.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284503 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284508 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=3$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=3,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284539 :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284546 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{z}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}\n$$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,z=t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{z}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{z}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(t{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =t+t+1 \\\\  & =2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284547 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [1,2].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t,\\,z={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\sqrt{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{t}+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{t}+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(2\\sqrt{t}+2{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3/2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}+8-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}+37}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284584 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2xy+{z}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3y{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3},y=1,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\mathbf{i}+(0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\mathbf{i}+(0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3}\\cdot 0+3{t}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284585 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{t}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}(t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\pi ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)+0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284586 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t+{cos}^{2}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284587 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ at the point $t=9.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right),\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=\\sqrt{t}$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(9))] & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284590 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em},$ for $0\\le t\\le 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2t\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{4y+1}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284591 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}1+t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}1+t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$    \n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =t+(1+t)=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2t+1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{[{t}^{2}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1+1-0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284592 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em},$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$, then along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y={t}^{3}.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(3t)^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =27{t}^{3}+{t}^{3} \\\\  & =28{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}28{t}^{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{4},$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}84{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}21\\cdot {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =14({2}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =14(2\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284593 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+16{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-16$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2(4\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}-16 \\\\  & =16(2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}16\\operatorname{cos}2t\\cdot 4\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =32[\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}0] \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284666 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x={t}^{2},$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot 2t+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284667 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284668 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy,$ where $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t(-\\operatorname{sin}t)+(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}s)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+\\operatorname{sin}s\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284669 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ where $x=t+s$ and $y=ts.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(ts{e}^{t+s})(1)+({e}^{t+s})(t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t+s}(s+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284670 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ where $x=s+t,$ $y={s}^{2}$ and $z={t}^{2}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{{c}_{1}{s}^{2}+{c}_{2}st+{c}_{3}{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y+z}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}\\cdot (1)+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{-({s}^{2}+{t}^{2})+2t(s+t)}{(s+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2ts-{s}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=-1,$ ${c}_{2}=2$ and ${c}_{3}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=-1+2+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284678 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $\\frac{1}{3},$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284679 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ y & =2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284680 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284681 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $4,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=4,\\,\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ x & =4\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ y & =4\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\ y & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2,-2\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284682 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $7,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=7,\\,\\theta =0.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ x & =7\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ y & =7\\cdot 0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(7,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284783 :question/problem "![](images/f1/f11e50891625fb39de472762aaaff23edf43372e37fcdf55d129da1125c0ff8a.png)\n\nFind the area of $△ABC.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}с & =AB=26, \\\\ b & =AC=22, \\\\ m∠A & ={11}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{11}^{∘}=0.190\\,809,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 22\\cdot 26\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{11}^{∘} \\\\  & =286\\cdot (0.190\\hspace{0.167em}809) \\\\  & \\approx 54.57,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287820 :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $p$ given that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 2p-4\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 2p-4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 2p-4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 2p-4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2p-4+10)-0+(-10-6) \\\\  & =2p-4+10-10-6 \\\\  & =2p-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0.$ So,  we have:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-10 & =0 \\\\ 2p & =10 \\\\ p & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287821 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n is $81,$ find all the possible values of $y.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{vmatrix} & =4\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ y & -5\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}-3y & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-3y & -2 \\\\ 2 & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (10-y)-2\\cdot (15y-2)+3\\cdot (-3{y}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =40-4y-30y+4-9{y}^{2}+12 \\\\  & =-9{y}^{2}-34y+56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $81,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{y}^{2}-34y+56 & =81 \\\\ -9{y}^{2}-34y-25 & =0 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+34y+25 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{{34}^{2}-4\\cdot 9\\cdot 25}}{2\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm \\sqrt{256}}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-34\\pm 16}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{-17\\pm 8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{25}{9}$ and $y=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287822 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-3,3,1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨0,4,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3((-1)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3)-3(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)+(2\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =39-6+8 \\\\  & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|41|=41.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287823 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors  $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,-1,-{k}^{2}⟩,\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,4,8⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-1,-1,5⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & -{k}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+(-{k}^{2})\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+(2\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 8)-{k}^{2}(2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =18-2{k}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ 18-2{k}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{k}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =9 \\\\ k & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-3$ or $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287824 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=-30,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-k & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-k\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2k & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-k\\cdot 3+0-3\\cdot (-6k) \\\\  & =-3k+18k \\\\  & =15k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-30,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15k & =-30 \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
